{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. Both halves, the comment correction, the architecture page stating the property rather than the mechanism, regressions at the real boundary with mutation proof, then review from codex. I will not merge.\n\nYOUR CONTRACT RULING IS BETTER THAN EITHER OPTION I OFFERED, and I want to say why rather than just accept it. I framed it as a choice between raising the code to the sentence or lowering the sentence to the code. You rejected both framings: state the property, not the mechanism. The reason that matters is precise — a page that says \"every variable that can redirect or reinterpret is stripped\" is a claim about a list, and a list is exactly what my judgement already failed at once tonight. A page that says the guard refuses whenever it cannot positively confirm is a claim about an outcome, and it stays true when a key nobody anticipated gets through. I would not have arrived at that on my own, and I had the evidence for it in my hands.\n\nWHAT I WILL BUILD. The exit-code distinction is load-bearing: git grep exit 1 means no matches and is the ordinary answer; anything else means the lookup did not run and must reach the caller as a refusal, not an empty set. Environment and configuration bounding on the constructor is the secondary defence, and I will write the comment to say which is which, so the next person does not repair the secondary one and think the class is closed.\n\nTHE COMMENT I HAVE TO CORRECT IS AN ARGUMENT, not a description, which is why it produced the defect. It reasons that a guard failing closed on its own malfunction would make a broken git a broken workroom, and treats that as sufficient reason to fail open. The premise is true and the conclusion does not follow for this guard: a broken git should stop a retirement, because the cost of stopping is a retry and the cost of passing is a retirement the documentation still cites. I will state that trade explicitly rather than just inverting the behaviour.\n\nWHAT I WILL PROVE, not assert. A regression at the real citingDocuments and retirement boundary showing the retirement is refused when command-scope configuration breaks the lookup, using the exact reproduction codex found and I confirmed — grep.threads=-1, exit 128 — and a second pinning that exit 1 still means no matches and still permits the retirement. Both mutation-checked: break the distinction, watch the named assertion fail, restore. A test that passes for the wrong reason would be worse here than no test, because it would look like this class was closed.\n\nONE THING I WILL WATCH FOR. Preserving what the GIT_CONFIG family is legitimately used for. gitstore and testgit pin the system and global config to /dev/null through it, and safe.directory normally lives in that scope. If bounding the family turns out to break either, I will stop and assert rather than weaken the pin, because that would be trading one fail-open for another."}